## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of crash‑consistency in non‑volatile memory, proper cache‑line flush semantics, and cross‑socket ordering. It probes how to design durable queues and recoveries with minimal latency impact.

## Key Concepts
- PMEM durability and flush/fence ordering (clwb/clflushopt, sfence)
- Release/acquire memory ordering and per‑slot sequencing
- Crash recovery and replay correctness in a bounded buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo enqueue (high level, non‑blocking semantics)
function enqueue(slot, payload){
  slot.payload = payload; // write payload
  slot.len = payload.length;
  slot.seq = slot.seq + 1; // release semantics
  clwb(&slot.payload); clwb(&slot.len); clwb(&slot.seq);
  sfence();
}
```

```javascript
// Pseudo dequeue
function dequeue(){
  if (ring.head.seq == ring.tail.seq) return null; // empty
  let s = ring.head; let payload = s.payload;
  // advance head after acquire read
  ring.head = next(s);
  s.done = true; clwb(&s.done); sfence();
  return payload;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

A practical test of low-level concurrency in a tiny allocator, exposing understanding of lock-free data structures, ABA avoidance, memory reclamation, and correctness across threads.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free free-list using CAS
- ABA avoidance (tag counters or epoch)
- Fixed-size pool management and memory reclamation
- Debug/test patterns for multithreaded correctness

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct Block { struct Block *next; } Block;

typedef struct Pool {
  Block *head;
  unsigned int tag;
} Pool;

Block *allocate(Pool *p) {
  Block *old = p->head;
  while (old) {
    Block *next = old->next;
    if (__atomic_compare_exchange_n(&p->head, &old, next, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)) {
      return old;
    }
  }
  return NULL;
}
void free_block(Pool *p, Block *b) {
  Block *old;
  do {
    old = p->head;
    b->next = old;
  } while (!__atomic_compare_exchange_n(&p->head, &old, b, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST));
}
```

